&ldquo;There are cases in detective history to show that criminals have been
      detected through peculiarities, in their appetites. Now, what does this
      elephant eat, and how much?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Everything that is fluid. Milk, water, whisky, molasses, castor oil,
      camphene, carbolic acid&mdash;it is no use to go into particulars;
      whatever fluid occurs to you set it down. He will drink anything that is
      fluid, except European coffee.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Place detectives in plain clothes in the railway; steamship, and ferry
      depots, and upon all roadways leading out of Jersey City, with orders to
      search all suspicious persons.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Furnish all these men with photograph and accompanying description of the
      elephant, and instruct them to search all trains and outgoing ferryboats
      and other vessels.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I see the force of what you say. But I noticed that in one part of your
      remarks in the papers this morning you refused to reveal your opinion upon
      a certain minor point.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I wish I could communicate with them and order them north, but that is
      impossible. A detective only visits a telegraph office to send his report;
      then he is off again, and you don't know where to put your hand on him.&rdquo;

&ldquo;There. We shall not have to wait long for an answer. Mr. Barnum is not at
      home; he is in the telegraph office&mdash;it is his way when he has
      business on hand. Inside of three&mdash;&rdquo;

Four more dull days followed. Then came a bitter blow to the poor,
      hard-working detectives&mdash;the journalists declined to print their
      theories, and coldly said, &ldquo;Give us a rest.&rdquo;
